course: dictionary
lesson: 7089
title: Money words in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can be easier to understand when you read the words around it. These clues are called context. «A payslip shows what you earned and what was taken off, such as tax and pension, before the rest reached your bank.» The words earned, taken off and bank tell you that a payslip is a note about money you were paid.
explain: Some words in a sentence are clues to each other. «Gross pay is the big number at the top of the payslip; net or take-home pay is what is actually left to spend.» Big number at the top is gross pay, and what is left to spend is net pay. The word left shows that net pay is the smaller amount, after things have been taken off.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Dad's payslip says he earned £2,500, but £2,000 arrived in the bank. The £500 that went missing was taken off for tax, which pays for schools, hospitals and roads. So a payslip shows what was earned and what was taken away before the money arrived.
fact: A payslip is a paper or digital note that an employer gives a worker with their pay.
fact: «Payslip» means a note showing how much a worker earned and what was taken off.
fact: Gross pay is the full amount earned, before anything is taken off.
fact: Net pay, or take-home pay, is the amount left after tax and other things are taken off.
fact: In America a payslip is often called a pay stub.
exercise: «A payslip shows what you earned and what was taken off, such as tax and pension.» What is a payslip? | *A note from an employer showing pay and what was taken off | A ticket for a train | A bill from the shop for things you bought | A map of the bank
exercise: «Gross pay is the big number at the top of the payslip.» What does gross pay mean? | *The full amount earned, before anything is taken off | The number of hours worked | The tax paid to the government | The amount left to spend
exercise: Dad's payslip says he earned £2,500, but £2,000 arrived. What was taken off? | Schools and hospitals | £2,000 | Nothing, the bank made a mistake | *£500 for tax
test: Which words mean the money left after tax and other things are taken off? | *Net pay, or take-home pay | Gross pay | Tax | Monthly payslip
test: Where might you find a payslip today? | In a football | *On paper or on a screen | In the sky | A pizza box
exam: Why might a bank ask to see a payslip before giving a mortgage? | To find out their favourite colour | To count the coins in the till | *To check how much someone earns | Because banks like paper
